{"type":"frame-snapshot","snapshot":{"callId":"zyfb@64","phase":"action","pageId":"page@548dbef582ab035dcbe6597195c569e8","frameId":"frame@bf0a36a90708cb1fd33ff13ee6282b10","frameUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:4173/","doctype":"html","html":["HTML",{"__playwright_scroll_top_":"105","lang":"en"},["HEAD",{},[[11,0]],[[11,1]],[[11,2]],[[11,3]],[[11,4]],[[11,5]],[[11,6]],[[11,7]],[[11,8]],[[11,9]],[[11,10]],[[11,11]],[[2,1]],[[11,14]],[[11,15]],[[11,16]],[[11,17]],[[2,2]],[[2,3]],[[2,4]],[[2,5]],["LINK",{"rel":"modulepreload","as":"script","crossorigin":"","href":"/assets/NearInspection-D5Tr5SzC.js"}],["LINK",{"rel":"modulepreload","as":"script","crossorigin":"","href":"/assets/SpacecraftInspection-CLN4DTMI.js"}],["LINK",{"rel":"modulepreload","as":"script","crossorigin":"","href":"/assets/SpacecraftViewer-BS9PZG-H.js"}],["LINK",{"rel":"modulepreload","as":"script","crossorigin":"","href":"/assets/OrbitControls-B480Y2e1.js"}],["LINK",{"rel":"modulepreload","as":"script","crossorigin":"","href":"/assets/Line-NwQCY_og.js"}],["LINK",{"rel":"modulepreload","as":"script","crossorigin":"","href":"/assets/constants-B-vUpRSd.js"}],["LINK",{"rel":"modulepreload","as":"script","crossorigin":"","href":"/assets/nearStore-B5r466W2.js"}],["LINK",{"rel":"modulepreload","as":"script","crossorigin":"","href":"/assets/store-C5wUW850.js"}],["LINK",{"rel":"modulepreload","as":"script","crossorigin":"","href":"/assets/NearJourney-BNEyuTDA.js"}]],[[11,19]],["BODY",{},[[11,20]],["DIV",{"id":"root"},["DIV",{"class":"app-shell","data-reduced-motion":"true"},[[11,22]],[[5,5]],["MAIN",{"id":"main","class":"main-content "},["SECTION",{"class":"interior-screen"},[[5,7]],[[8,10]],[[2,8]],["DIV",{"class":"prose"},["P",{"class":"eyebrow"},"433 EROS / 1996–2001"],["P",{"class":"lead"},"It had spent a year learning to stay above the rock. Now the team wanted to bring it down."],["P",{},"Recover the route, the measurements and the deliberate decision that turned an orbiter into a surviving spacecraft on the surface. Six investigations await."],["BUTTON",{"__playwright_target__":"","class":"primary-button"},"Inspect NEAR Shoemaker"],["DETAILS",{"class":"listen-mode"},["SUMMARY",{},["svg",{"aria-hidden":"true","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","width":"22","height":"22"},["path",{"d":"M4 15V9a8 8 0 0116 0v6l-3 5H7zM7 8h10v7H7z","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","stroke-width":"1.5"}]]," Helmet Listen Mode"],["P",{},"Listen to ","NEAR briefing",". Original text stays available. Browser voices vary; word-by-word synchronization is not provided."],["P",{},"Audio is muted. ",["BUTTON",{"type":"button"},"Unmute audio"]],["DIV",{"class":"listen-controls"},["BUTTON",{"type":"button"},"Listen / Replay"],["BUTTON",{"type":"button","disabled":""},"Pause narration"],["BUTTON",{"type":"button"},"Stop narration"]],["LABEL",{"for":"_r_1_"},"Narration speed"],["SELECT",{"id":"_r_1_"},["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"false","value":"0.75"},"0.75","×"],["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"true","value":"1"},"1","×"],["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"false","value":"1.25"},"1.25","×"],["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"false","value":"1.5"},"1.5","×"]],["LABEL",{},"Voice volume",["INPUT",{"__playwright_value_":"1","aria-label":"Voice volume","min":"0","max":"1","step":"0.1","type":"range","value":"1"}]],["P",{"role":"status"},"Stopped"]],["BUTTON",{"class":"text-button"},"Watch mission film"],["DETAILS",{"class":"mission-reference"},["SUMMARY",{},"NEAR Shoemaker"," — complete records & sources"],["P",{},"Read the full mission record at any time. These reference pages do not collect quest evidence or unlock objectives. Dates describe historical events; illustrations explain concepts."],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"A rendezvous takes more than a straight line"],["P",{},"NEAR launched on February 17, 1996, passed Mathilde on June 27, 1997 and used an Earth gravity assist on January 23, 1998. A near-Earth asteroid follows a solar orbit that brings it into the neighbourhood of Earth; it does not mean an impact is imminent. Navigation changes the spacecraft trajectory to meet a moving target. Mathilde was a flyby; Eros was the rendezvous objective."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"The meeting that had to wait"],["P",{},"An aborted December 20, 1998 engine burn prevented the intended rendezvous. A December 23 flyby gathered observations before a revised trajectory brought NEAR back in 2000. A flyby passes a target. Rendezvous reduces relative motion enough to stay nearby."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"Not a small planet"],["P",{},"The real Eros shape is elongated and irregular. The supplied NASA STL preserves surface relief but contains no photographic texture, geographic axes or longitude labels. Rotate the body to compare silhouettes. Shape alone cannot identify mineral composition or justify naming an unregistered crater."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/3d-resources/asteroid-433-eros/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA Eros shape"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"Mass is not arranged like a sphere"],["P",{},"Irregular shape and internal mass distribution affect the gravity field. Navigation used radio range and Doppler measurements alongside spacecraft observations. A uniform spherical teaching model cannot reproduce Eros operations. Measurements and planned corrections matter."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://pds.nasa.gov/ds-view/pds/viewProfile.jsp?dsid=NEAR-A-RSS-1%2F5-EROS%2FORBIT-V1.0","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA PDS radio science archive"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"Stay nearby, then look closer"],["P",{},"NEAR entered orbit on February 14, 2000. The team changed orbits for different observations: by April 30 the operational distance was about 50 km from the centre; a July phase used about 35 km from the centre. Centre distance is not surface clearance. An irregular surface changes the gap underneath an orbit. The displayed loops are schematic, not measured trajectories."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"Pictures and reflected light"],["P",{},"The Multi-Spectral Imager mapped morphology and colour differences. The Near Infrared Spectrometer measured reflected infrared light to investigate minerals. A May 2000 power problem ended NIS operations. A photograph can reveal structure; spectral measurements help interpret materials. No single view supplies every answer."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jhuapl.edu/destinations/missions/near","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"JHUAPL spacecraft and teams"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"Six ways to ask about a rock"],["P",{},"X-ray/gamma-ray spectroscopy probes elemental composition. The laser rangefinder measures distance for shape and topography. The magnetometer investigates magnetic fields. Radio tracking constrains motion, gravity and mass distribution. Combine complementary measurements rather than assigning every result to a camera. Radio science uses communications tracking, not a separate camera."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jhuapl.edu/destinations/missions/near","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"JHUAPL spacecraft and teams"]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://pds.nasa.gov/ds-view/pds/viewProfile.jsp?dsid=NEAR-A-RSS-1%2F5-EROS%2FORBIT-V1.0","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA PDS radio science archive"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"An orbiter, deliberately brought down"],["P",{},"The spacecraft had no purpose-built landing gear. After a year of orbital study, planners chose a slow controlled descent on February 12, 2001. It photographed the surface on the way down and survived contact. Not designed as a lander does not mean an accidental crash. A planned descent managed risk without guaranteeing survival."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jhuapl.edu/destinations/missions/near","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"JHUAPL spacecraft and teams"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"South of Himeros"],["P",{},"NASA describes touchdown south of the saddle-shaped Himeros region. The official landing-site mosaic shows the planned site with south toward the top. This STL has no verified registration to the published geographic frame. Use the official image for regional context; there is no exact geographic pin on our mesh."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/location-of-the-near-shoemaker-landing-site/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA/JPL/JHUAPL landing-site image"]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"The signal after contact"],["P",{},"The orbiter survived touchdown. On February 14 NASA announced an extension for gamma-ray measurements and prioritised their recovery over other recorder data using the reliable low-gain link. Close surface proximity offered a valuable opportunity to investigate surface and subsurface composition. A working link and careful data priorities made survival scientifically useful."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/near-shoemaker-ready-to-gather-data-on-surface-of-eros/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA surface science announcement"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"The last transmission was not the last result"],["P",{},"Last contact was February 28, 2001. NEAR established asteroid orbiting and landing milestones and left a record of images, spectra, ranging and tracking for later study. The mission made small-body operations a practical engineering experience. It did not collect or return asteroid samples."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jhuapl.edu/destinations/missions/near","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"JHUAPL spacecraft and teams"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"People, teams and credits"],["P",{},"NASA funded the Discovery mission. Johns Hopkins APL built and operated NEAR; Thomas Coughlin was project manager, Robert Farquhar mission manager and Andrew Cheng project scientist. The name honours planetary geologist Eugene Shoemaker. Models are supplied NASA resources. Landing-site image credit: NASA/JPL/JHUAPL. Independent project; no NASA endorsement."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://www.jhuapl.edu/destinations/missions/near","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"JHUAPL spacecraft and teams"]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/3d-resources/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA NEAR model"]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/3d-resources/asteroid-433-eros/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA Eros shape"]],["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/location-of-the-near-shoemaker-landing-site/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA/JPL/JHUAPL landing-site image"]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"Mission timeline"],["OL",{"class":"reference-timeline"},["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"1996-02-17"],["P",{},"Launch from Earth"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"1997-06-27"],["P",{},"Mathilde flyby"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"1998-01-23"],["P",{},"Earth gravity assist"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"1998-12-23"],["P",{},"Initial Eros flyby after the aborted burn"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"2000-02-14"],["P",{},"Eros orbital insertion"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"2000-04-30"],["P",{},"Operational orbit about 50 km from centre"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"2000-07-13"],["P",{},"Lower orbital phase about 35 km from centre"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"2001-02-12"],["P",{},"Controlled descent and touchdown south of Himeros"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"2001-02-14"],["P",{},"Surface gamma-ray science extension announced"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/near-shoemaker-ready-to-gather-data-on-surface-of-eros/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA surface science announcement"]]]],["LI",{},["STRONG",{},"2001-02-28"],["P",{},"Last contact"],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"]]]]]],["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"Detailed engineering and evidence records"],["DIV",{"class":"reference-records"},["DETAILS",{},["SUMMARY",{},"A rendezvous takes more than a straight line"],["P",{"class":"record-context"},"NEAR Shoemaker / 433 Eros"],["P",{},"NEAR launched on February 17, 1996, passed Mathilde on June 27, 1997 and used an Earth gravity assist on January 23, 1998. A near-Earth asteroid follows a solar orbit that brings it into the neighbourhood of Earth; it does not mean an impact is imminent."],["SECTION",{},["H4",{},"Why this matters"],["P",{},"Navigation changes the spacecraft trajectory to meet a moving target. Mathilde was a flyby; Eros was the rendezvous objective."]],["P",{"class":"record-relevance"},["STRONG",{},"Why it matters:"]," ","Navigation changes the spacecraft trajectory to meet a moving target. Mathilde was a flyby; Eros was the rendezvous objective."],["UL",{"class":"evidence-sources"},["LI",{},["A",{"href":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer"},"NASA mission history"